--TEST--
`Core\Xml::parse` expands the five predefined entities and numeric character references, which are the document's own characters written another way, and resolves no other entity from anywhere — `rule:core-classes/html-parsing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The concatenation of every text child, which is where an element's characters
// live: a node's `text()` is its own, never a walk over its descendants.
class Chars {
    public static function of(Core\Xml\Node $element): tainted string {
        tainted string $out = "";
        foreach ($element->children() as Core\Xml\Node $child) {
            if ($child->kind() == Core\Xml\NodeKind::Text) {
                $out = $out . $child->text();
            }
        }
        return $out;
    }
}

class Root {
    public static function of(Core\Xml\Node $document): Core\Xml\Node {
        foreach ($document->children() as Core\Xml\Node $child) {
            if ($child->kind() == Core\Xml\NodeKind::Element) {
                return $child;
            }
        }
        return $document;
    }
}

// The five, then a decimal and a hexadecimal character reference. Each names a
// character the document could have written directly, which is the whole of why
// it is expanded rather than looked up.
Core\Xml\Node $five = Core\Xml::parse("<t>&amp;&lt;&gt;&quot;&apos;|&#65;&#x42;</t>");
echo "five: ", Chars::of(Root::of($five)), "\n";

// A CDATA section is text, and text is text: the same characters reach the
// program whichever way the document spelled them.
Core\Xml\Node $cdata = Core\Xml::parse("<t><![CDATA[a & <b>]]>|a &amp; &lt;b&gt;</t>");
echo "cdata: ", Chars::of(Root::of($cdata)), "\n";

// The same expansion inside an attribute value, which is the other place a
// reference may be written.
Core\Xml\Node $attributes = Core\Xml::parse("<t k=\"&lt;&#38;&gt;\"/>");
foreach (Root::of($attributes)->attributes() as tainted string $name => tainted string $value) {
    echo "attribute ", $name, " = ", $value, "\n";
}

// `&nbsp;` is HTML's, and it is not resolved here — not from a document type
// declaration, not from an internal subset, and not from the network. There is
// no branch in this parser that could have fetched one.
try {
    Core\Xml::parse("<t>&nbsp;</t>");
    echo "an entity was resolved from somewhere\n";
} catch (ParseError $undeclared) {
    echo $undeclared->message, "\n";
}

// A bare ampersand is refused rather than read as itself: a document that means
// one writes `&amp;`, and guessing which was meant is repair.
try {
    Core\Xml::parse("<t>Smith & Sons</t>");
    echo "a bare ampersand was read as itself\n";
} catch (ParseError $bare) {
    echo $bare->message, "\n";
}

// A numeric reference that names no character a document may hold is refused
// for the same reason: it is not a character written another way.
try {
    Core\Xml::parse("<t>&#0;</t>");
    echo "a null was written into a document\n";
} catch (ParseError $nothing) {
    echo $nothing->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
five: &<>"'|AB
cdata: a & <b>|a & <b>
attribute k = <&>
Core\Xml::parse(): `&nbsp;` is not one of the five predefined entities, and no entity is resolved from a document type declaration, an internal subset or the network.
Core\Xml::parse(): `& Sons</t>` opens a reference that is never closed by `;` — a literal ampersand is written `&amp;`.
Core\Xml::parse(): `&#0;` names no character a document may hold.
